## Question 1: What is a row?
Each row of the PDHS 2017-18 Children's Recode file (PKKR71FL.DTA) is one child born to an interviewed woman; the file has 12,708 rows and 1,186 columns (`df.shape`). The codebook labels `caseid` "Case Identification" and `midx` "Index to Birth History". In my data two rows share the same caseid ("1 9 4") but have midx 1 and 2, so one woman appears in several rows, once per child. This means rows are not independent (siblings share a mother and household), which matters when I split data into train and test. The file also includes children recorded as dead (719 rows in `hw13`), so a row is a birth, not only a living child. [TO CHECK: the age window of children in this file, from the DHS recode manual or final report.] Confidence: high that one row is one child, medium on the exact age window.

## Question 2: What is the unit of the outcome?
The outcome is child stunting, based on `hw70`, which the codebook labels "Height/Age standard deviation (new WHO)". A child is stunted if the z-score is below -2. The values look stored in hundredths of a standard deviation: the median in my data is -149 and the minimum -599, consistent with z-scores of about -1.49 and -5.99 [TO CHECK: confirm the x100 storage in the DHS recode manual]. So the rule I will use is `hw70 < -200`. The codebook defines three special codes: 9996 "Height out of plausible limits", 9997 "Age in days out of plausible limits" and 9998 "Flagged cases". In my data 85 records carry 9998, and none carry 9996 or 9997. These are not real scores, so I plan to treat them as missing, leaving 4,141 valid scores (4,226 minus 85); I will record this decision in CLEANING.md. Confidence: high for the definition and the special codes (codebook), medium for the scale until I check the manual.

## Question 3: What is the timeframe?
The data covers interviews conducted between November 2017 and April 2018. The v007 column shows 3,723 interviews in 2017 and 8,985 in 2018, and the month column v006 shows 375 in November, 3,348 in December, then 3,898, 3,142, 1,685 and 260 for January to April. The monthly counts add up to the yearly ones, so the file is internally consistent. The DHS Program’s survey presentation gives the fieldwork period as 22 November 2017 to 30 April 2018, which matches my data. This is a single snapshot, so the model predicts current stunting, not change over time, and it covers only the winter fieldwork months. Confidence: high.



